In his 1993 book *Usability Engineering*, Jakob Nielsen described three response-time limits that he traced to earlier research. About 0.1 seconds feels instantaneous. About 1 second keeps the user’s flow of thought unbroken. About 10 seconds is the most you get before attention wanders. Those numbers come from human perception, so they have not moved as hardware has.

## What the browser measures now

Google’s Core Web Vitals turned part of this into a metric. Interaction to Next Paint, or INP, replaced First Input Delay in March 2024. A page is rated good when INP is 200 milliseconds or less at the 75th percentile of visits, and poor above 500 milliseconds.

Notice that 200 ms is twice Nielsen’s limit. The metric is a pass mark for a whole page, not a target for a button that should feel immediate. Treat it as the ceiling.

## Spend the budget on purpose

A tap that has to feel instant gets about 100 ms for everything: input handling, state updates, layout and paint. Work out where those milliseconds go before you write the handler. Then put anything slow, such as a network call or a long list, behind visible feedback inside that first 100 ms.

```javascript
const t0 = performance.now();
render(items);
requestAnimationFrame(() => {
  const ms = performance.now() - t0;
  if (ms > 100) console.warn('render took ' + Math.round(ms) + ' ms');
});
```

The sketch above is a rough gauge. It stops at the next frame, so it misses part of paint and compositing. The Performance panel in your browser’s developer tools shows the full picture.

## Measure first, then argue

Most arguments about speed end quickly once someone records a trace. Record one on a mid-range phone, not on the laptop you build with, and keep the budget visible where the whole team will see it.

A site with a handful of views does not need a routing library. The part of a URL after the `#` never reaches the server, so hash links work on any host with no configuration. Every change also adds a history entry, so the back button behaves the way people expect.

## The whole router

Read `location.hash`, look the key up in a table, render, and listen for `hashchange`. That is the entire mechanism.

```javascript
const routes = {
  home: renderHome,
  about: renderAbout,
};

function route() {
  const key = location.hash.slice(1) || 'home';
  (routes[key] || renderNotFound)();
  window.scrollTo(0, 0);
}

addEventListener('hashchange', route);
route();
```

## Three details that matter

First, reset the scroll position after each render, because a new view should open at the top. Second, move focus to the new view’s main heading so keyboard and screen reader users land where sighted users do. Third, update `document.title` so history entries and browser tabs describe where you are.

## Where it stops

Hash routing suits a few named views and a lookup by id. Once you need nested layouts, data loading per route or many dynamic paths, a proper router earns its weight. Until then, a short function you can read in one sitting is easier to keep.

A focus ring is the visible marker that shows keyboard and switch users which control will respond when they press a key. Removing it with `outline: none` and offering nothing in its place makes a page close to unusable for them.

## Use focus-visible

The `:focus-visible` pseudo-class shows the ring when the browser judges that it helps, which in practice means keyboard focus and not mouse clicks. It removes the usual reason designers give for hiding outlines.

```css
:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid #e9b96e;
  outline-offset: 3px;
}
```

## Make it visible against the ground

A ring needs contrast with whatever sits behind it. WCAG 2.1 asks for 3 to 1 against adjacent colors for interface components, under criterion 1.4.11, Non-text Contrast. On a dark page a light accent works well. Offset the ring a few pixels so it does not merge with the control’s own border.

## Do not let the header cover it

WCAG 2.2 added criterion 2.4.11, Focus Not Obscured (Minimum), at level AA. A sticky header can hide a control that has just received focus. The fix is usually one line.

```css
html {
  scroll-padding-top: 5.5rem; /* sticky header height, plus a little */
}
```

This site uses exactly that. Press Tab through any page and watch where the ring lands.
